## Highlights
- Novel magnetic MOFs-Starch hydrogel has been synthesized.
- High surface area \((528.39\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) of the MOFs-Starch hydrogel was confirmed.
- Maximum swelling ratio \((1000.0\%)\) of the hydrogel at pH 10, 180 min and \(25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\).
- Adsorptive removal of Fluvastatin statin drug was optimized.
- Adsorption was best fitted with Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-second models.
Abstract
Growing interests and efforts have been recently focused on design and assembly of novel hydrogel nanosorbents for removal of drugs from wastewater. Therefore, this work is aimed to immobilize and encapsulate starch hydrogel matrix onto metal organic frameworks (MOFs) and dope with nanomagnetite. The
magnetic MOFs-Starch hydrogel (NFe3O4@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel) was synthesized via microwave irradiation process and characterized with high surface area (528.39 m2/g), mesoporous with pore size 2.90 nm and highly crystalline structure. The maximum swelling ratio (1000.0%) was optimized at pH 10, 180 min and 25oC. The validity of NFe3O4@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel for adsorptive removal of Fluvastatin statin drug provided maximum equilibrium adsorption capacity 782.05 mg g-1. The Langmuir isotherm and pseudo-second kinetics models were correlated well with the computed correlation coefficient values 0.9991 and 0.9997, respectively. The validity of NFe3O4@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel for removal of FLV statin drug from real water matrices was confirmed in the range 96.15-99.99%.
Keywords: Fluvastatin drug; Starch hydrogel; MOFs; Magnetite nanoparticles; Factors optimization; Kinetics-isotherm-Thermodynamics study.
## 1. Introduction
Fluvastatin (FLV) is a synthetic inhibitor for 3-hydroxy-3-methylglutaryl coenzyme A reductase, which is known to act as the rate limiting enzyme for cholesterol biosynthesis in the liver and is commonly used to improve lipid profiles in patients with primary hypercholesterolemia and mixed dyslipidemia (Mathew and Rajagopal, 2017; Dietrich et al., 2002; Fabbri, S.Franzellitti, 2016). (Dai et al., 2020). Due to their prevalent use, resistance to hydrolysis and confined human metabolism, statin drug excessively detected in surface water effluents (Nassar et al., 2019; Sarker et al., 2018; Janssens et al., 2017). Laboratory exposures to environmentally relevant concentrations have produced effects in fish and plants, resembling pathology observed in wild populations (Kowalski et al., 2006). Recent laboratory research has reported that small amounts of medication
in drinking water can affect human embryonic kidney cells, human blood cells and human breast cancer cells (Razavi et al., 2011; Tete et al., 2019; Santos et al., 2016).
Removal of these bio-accumulative pollutants from water is a critical issue to overcome the serious effect of these pollutants on water quality (Pandey et al., 2020a; Pandey et al., 2020b; Namazi et al., 2014; An et al., 2011). Hydrogel is three-dimensional networks constructed by physical or chemical crosslinking of polymer chains. The porous three-dimensional structure and polar functional groups endorse hydrogel outstanding performances on scavenging drugs (Javanbakht et al., 2019; Pooresmaeil et al., 2018). Specifically, the porous structure renders hydrogel high specific surface areas, enabling large numbers of adsorption sites exposed to target drugs (Fang et al., 2018). The dense functional groups are capable of binding drugs (Takei et al., 2020). In addition, hydrogel can absorb water without losing its structural integrity, which facilitates the separation and recycling of the hydrogel adsorbents from treated effluents. Therefore, in the past decade, hydrogel has become a well-known and promising adsorbent for sequestrating drugs from polluted water (Pooresmaeil et al., 2020). A three-dimensional graphene porous hydrogel was investigated with respect to the ciprofloxacin adsorption mechanism and moisture contents impact (Yu et al., 2019). A study for enhanced adsorptive removal of some antibiotics from aqueous solutions was reported by the use of triple-network nanocomposite hydrogels from a combination of carbon nanotubes-graphene oxide-sodium alginate (Ma et al., 2020). Adsorption and photocatalytic degradation of sulfamethoxazole drug by a novel synthesized (p(HEA/NMMA)-CuS) hydrogel catalyst using irradiation polymerization and in-situ precipitation methods was successfully studied (Yang et al., 2017). On the other hand, Starch-Hydrogels have various potential properties as biodegradability, biocompatibility, low cost as well as non-toxicity. Starch is the most abundant polysaccharide stored in plants (Namazi and
Mosadegh 2011). It consists of distinct units of glucose molecules linked together with \(\alpha\)-D (1–4) and \(\alpha\)-D (1–6) bonds. Starch-hydrogels have a variety of applications as drug delivery, food preservative, water treatment, tissue engineering as well as personal care products (Ismail et al., 2012).
Another important class of porous materials is known as the metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) due to their attractive tuneability, high flexibility, good functionalities and porous structures (Mahmoud et al., 2020a). These materials have been recently confirmed with excellent advantages in research fields including adsorption and catalysis processes (Liu et al., 2019; Latha et al., 2020; Javanbakht et al., 2018; Salgaonkar et al., 2018). Al-MOFs was synthesized and investigated for aqueous solution treatment from naproxen drug (Li et al., 2020). Adsorption and recovery of levofloxacin drug onto iron based MIL-100(Fe) MOFs from aqueous medium was recently explored and reported (Chaturvedi et al., 2020). The functionalization method of poly(4-vinylpyridine) with magnetic copper as an effective and recyclable adsorbent for removal of fluoroquinolone antibiotics from aqueous solutions was reported (Wu et al., 2018). A study was made and reported to investigate MOF-5 by the process of adsorption isotherm, kinetic and thermodynamic parameters for removal of tetracycline antibiotic from aqueous solutions (Mirsoleimani-azizi et al., 2018). Synthesis of activated carbon–HKUST-1-MOFs hybrid material by ultrasonically assisted hydrothermal approach and simultaneous ultrasound-assisted removal of antibacterial was investigated by Taguchi optimization (Azad et al., 2016).
Particularly, tailoring the chemical properties of hydrogels remains a barrier to the successful translation of hydrogels to specific applications, which is important to create high applied performance of functional materials. The 3D hydrogel networks can be used to promote the dispersibility of MOFs in hydrogel matrices. Introducing MOFs nanoparticles into a hydrogel polymer can cause various positive effects on the chemical structure and significantly change its
environmental applications, durability, adsorption capacity as well as mechanical properties. The present study is directed and focused on the assembly of a new nanocomposite based on the potential encapsulation of metal organic frameworks (Zn(GA)) into starch hydrogel matrices and these are doped with magnetic iron oxide \(\mathrm{(NFe_{3}O_{4})}\) using microwave irradiation process (Makhado et al., 2019) for the formation of a novel \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel}\). Moreover, metal organic frameworks are characterized by high surface area and thus it is expected to exhibit excellent adsorption capacity for removal of different pollutants from aqueous solution. On the other hand, hydrogels are also characterized by excellent adsorption capability due to their porous structure. Therefore, combination of these two materials (MOFs and hydrogel) in a new composite will incorporate and afford high surface area as well as porous structure for binding and removing different pollutants. The assembled hydrogel nanosorbent is directed to adsorb fluvastatin drug in presence of different experimental controlling factors and to evaluate the kinetics and adsorption isotherm models as well as thermodynamic parameters.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Chemicals and Materials
Iron (III) chloride \(\mathrm{(FeCl_{3},\ 99.9\%)}\), iron (II) chloride \(\mathrm{(FeCl_{2},\ 99.9\%)}\), hydrochloric acid (HCl, \(37\%\)), sodium hydroxide (NaOH, \(98.0\%\)), sodium chloride \(\mathrm{(NaCl, \geq 99.0\%)}\) and starch \(\mathrm{((C_{6}H_{10}O_{5})_{n})}\), (amylose \(17.5\%\), amylopectin \(82.5\%\), MW \(692.7\ \mathrm{g/mol}\), moisture content \(12.0\%\), total ash \(0.3\%\), assay \(99\%\)) were procured from Sigma Aldrich, USA. Sodium nitrate \(\mathrm{(NaNO_{3},\ 99.9\%)}\), sodium carbonate \(\mathrm{(Na_{2}CO_{3},\ 99.99\%)}\) and sodium bicarbonate \(\mathrm{(NaHCO_{3},\ 99\%)}\) were obtained from El-Nasr chemicals, Egypt. Fluvastatin \(\mathrm{(C_{24}H_{25}FNO_{4}\cdot Na\cdot xH_{2}O, \geq 98\%}\), Scheme S1) was obtained from the Nile for Pharmaceuticals Industry, Egypt. Zinc acetate \(\mathrm{(ZnCH_{3}COOH, 99.99\%)}\), glutaric acid \(\mathrm{(HOOC(CH_{2})_{3}COOH, 99\%)}\),
malonic acid \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{2}(\mathrm{COOH})_{2}, 99\%\right)\), sodium sulfate \(\left(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}, 99\%\right)\) were purchased from BDH Chemicals, England.
## 2.2. Synthesis
## 2.2.1. Synthesis of starch hydrogel
Starch powder \((2.0\ \mathrm{g})\) was dissolved in 20 mL acetic acid (10% w/w) and then added to malonic acid \((1.0\ \mathrm{g})\). The mixture was added to \(100\ \mathrm{mL}\) of deionized water, and stirred vigorously for \(3\ \mathrm{h}\), at room temperature. The resulting starch hydrosol was then added dropwise to \(1.0\ \mathrm{M}\) NaOH solution and stored for \(1\ \mathrm{h}\). After filtration, the starch hydrogel were rinsed with an excess of distilled water until a neutral \(\mathrm{pH}\) value was achieved. The produced starch based hydrogel was dried at \(50\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\). Scheme S2 demonstrates the possible physical binding (H-bonding) and chemical bond formation between starch and malonic acid cross-linker.
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of \(\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{GA})\)-Starch hydrogel
\(1.0\ \mathrm{g}\) of starch hydrogel was dissolved into \(100\ \mathrm{mL}\) distilled water under room temperature conditions and stirred for \(1\ \mathrm{h}\) to get homogeneous solution. Then, the starch solution was dropped into a \(100\ \mathrm{mL}\) mixture solution containing zinc acetate \((0.01\ \mathrm{mmol})\) and glutaric acid \((0.01\ \mathrm{mmol})\), and then stirred at room temperature for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\) followed by heating under microwave irradiation for \(3\ \mathrm{min}\). It was washed with ethanol twice and then dried at \(50\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C}\) (Mahmoud et al., 2019a). The prepared MOFs-hydrogel (Zn(GA)-Starch hydrogel) was based on both physical and chemical linkages via H-bonding and ester bond formation between MOFs molecules and polymeric starch matrices.
2.2.3. Synthesis of magnetic MOFs-starch hydrogel (NFe\(_3\)O\(_4\)\@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel)
Synthesis of magnetic iron oxide nanoparticles \(\mathrm{(NFe_{3}O_{4})}\) was accomplished by the following steps (Stan, et al., 2019a; Stan, et al., 2019b). A dissolved mixture of ferric chloride \((0.04\ \mathrm{mol})\) and ferrous chloride \((0.02\ \mathrm{mol})\) in HCl \((0.5\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1})\) was heated in the microwave for 2 min. Sodium hydroxide \((2.0\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{L}^{-1})\) was then added and heated in the microwave oven for 2 min, washed with distilled water and dried at \(60^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). On the other hand, the preparation of magnetic MOFs-Starch hydrogel \(\mathrm{(NFe_{3}O_{4}@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel)}\) was successfully accomplished by doping nanomagnetic iron oxide into the pores of \(\mathrm{Zn(GA)-Starch}\) hydrogel by grinding \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}}\) \((1.0\ \mathrm{g})\) with \(\mathrm{Zn(GA)-Starch}\) hydrogel \((2.0\ \mathrm{g})\) and microwave irradiated for 2.0 min. The prepared \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel}\) was based on the physical adsorption chemical binding via elimination of water molecules between doped \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}}\) and \(\mathrm{Zn(GA)/starch}\). Finally, \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel}\) was dried at \(50\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for 6 h (Scheme 1).
## 2.3. Characterization
Characterization of the assembled materials was performed by different techniques including FT-IR, SEM, XRD and BET surface area. The specifications of the applied techniques are compiled in Table S1.
## 2.4. Adsorption experiments
## 2.4.1. Factor optimization
The efficiency of FLV statin drug removal by \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel}\) was monitored under different conditions of \(\mathrm{pH}\), time, FLV statin drug concentration, \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel}\) dosage, ionic strength and temperature. Table S2 outlines the selected conditions for FLV removal by \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel}\). Briefly, \(20\ \mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel}\) was mixed with \(10\ \mathrm{mL}\) of FLV statin drug with definite concentration, shaking under certain speed until equilibrium, centrifuge and measure the remained
concentration of the desired FLV statin drug using UV–Vis spectrophotometer at \(\lambda_{\operatorname*{max}}=332\ \mathrm{nm}\). All experiments were repeated three times and the average value was computed to provide the removal efficiency (\% R) according to Eq. (1)
\[
\% \mathrm{R} = (\mathrm{C_i} - \mathrm{C_f}) / \mathrm{C_f} \times 100
\]
Where \(\mathrm{C_{i}}\) is initial concentration of FLV statin drug \((\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{~L^{-1}})\) and \(\mathrm{C_{f}}\) is the concentrations of FLV at equilibrium.
2.4.2. Application of \(\mathbf{NFe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel for FLV removal from real water matrices
The efficiency of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel for removal of FLV statin drug from real water was examined under optimized experimental conditions \((\mathrm{t}=30\ \mathrm{min},\ \mathrm{pH}=4\) and temperature \(=25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\). \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) of FLV statin drug (15, 25 and \(40~\mathrm{mg~L^{-1}}\)) already dissolved in samples of tap water, seawater and industrial wastewater were added to \(50~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel, shaking, centrifuging and the values of removal efficiency of FLV statin drug from tap water, seawater and industrial wastewater were determined as previously reported according to Eq (1).
## 2.5. Swelling ratio at distinct conditions
The swelling ratio (SRO) of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel was evaluated under different conditions of time, \(\mathrm{pH}\) and temperature. The impact of time on SRO was evaluated based on keeping the \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel. After the predetermined time the hydrogel was removed from the deionized water, and the excess of the adsorbed water was removed by filter paper and weighed and the SRO value was computed from Eq. 2. In addition, the impact of \(\mathrm{pH}\) on SRO was monitored by keeping the hydrogel in deionized water at \(\mathrm{pH}\) (2-10) for \(180~\mathrm{min}\). The impact of temperature on SRO was investigated by
immersing the hydrogel in deionized water at temperature (25, 30, 50, 60, 70 and \(80\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) for \(180\;\mathrm{min}\).
\[
\mathrm{SRO\%}=(\mathrm{W_S-W_D})/\mathrm{W_D}\times100\%
\]
Where SRO: swelling ratio, \(\mathrm{W_{S}}\) : weight of swollen \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Z n(G A)/S t}\) archHydrogel and \(\mathrm{W_{D}}\) : weight of dried \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Z n(G A)}\) -Starch Hydrogel.
## 2.6. Reusability of \(\mathbf{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Z n(G A)/S t}\) arch-Hydrogel
The recycling/reusability of the \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Z n(G A)/S t}\) arch-Hydrogel was monitored according to the following procedure. A mixture containing \(100~\mathrm{mg}\) of \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Z n(G A)/S t}\) arch-Hydrogel and \(10~\mathrm{mL}\) of FLV statin drug was adjusted at pH 2 and shaken at room temperature. Then, the FLV loaded\(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Z n(G A)/S t}\) arch-Hydrogel was rinsed with HCl solution, washed with distilled water and dried at \(60\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Mahmoud et al., 2020a).
The point-zero charge (pzc) was deduced by mixing 50 mg \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Z n(G A)/S t}\) arch-Hydrogel with \(50~\mathrm{mL}\) of KCl \((0.01\,\mathrm{\mol\L^{-1}})\) for tuning the ionic strength of solution. The pH was adjusted to \((\mathrm{pH}=2,3,4,...12)\), shaken automatically for 3h and finally, the final pH of the mixed solution was measured.
## 3. Result and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization of \(\mathbf{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Z n(G A)/S t}\) arch-Hydrogel
Fig. S1 represents the FT-IR spectra for the prepared starch hydrogel, Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel and \(\mathrm{NFe_{3}O_{4}@Z n(G A)/S t}\) arch-Hydrogel. For starch, the band at \(1647~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is due to adsorbed water in the amorphous regions of starch. The bands between \(1400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) and \(1348~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) are assigned to C-O-O stretching and C-O-H bending. The observed band at 1130 indicates C-O vibration. Bands are also observed at \(930~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (ascribed to C-O-C vibration of \(\alpha\)-1,4 glyosidic linkage) and \(862~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) (ascribed to \(\mathrm{CH}_{2}\) deformation) (Ngwabebhoh et al., 2016). In addition,
Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel was characterized by the peaks at 1670, 1400 and 1300 \(\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) that are related to \(\mathbf{C}{=}\mathbf{O}\) carboxylic, O-H bend as well as C-O stretch, respectively (Luo et al., 2019). On the other hand, the broad and strong peak at \(3400~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) is related to O-H stretching to emphasize the formed intermolecular H-bonding between MOFs and starch hydrogel. Otherwise, the successful doping of iron oxide nanoparticles into hydrogel pores was asserted by the peak at \(570~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) which is relate to Fe-O in \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) molecules (Lee et al., 2019).
The morphology of starch hydrogel, Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel and \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{St}\) arch-Hydrogel was investigated as demonstrated from the SEM technique. The identified particle size of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{St}\) arch-Hydrogel was 287.77-291.35 nm. Fig. 1 shows the network image of starch hydrogel which is quite different from the desired Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel and \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{St}\) arch-Hydrogel (Fig. 1) in which the binding between MOFs and starch hydrogel for formation of MOFs-based hydrogel as well as the doping \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) was obviously observed by the change in morphological image of the formed nanocomposite \((\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{St}\) arch-Hydrogel). On the other hand, Fig. S2 shows the morphology change in \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{St}\) arch-Hydrogel before and after FLV adsorption.
The XRD patterns of starch hydrogel, Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel and \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{St}\) arch-Hydrogel are demonstrated in Fig. 2. The XRD pattern of starch hydrogel has a two unique peaks at \(2\theta=18^{\circ}\) and \(30^{\circ}\), while the XRD of Zn(GA)/Starch-Hydrogel showed a new peaks at \(2\theta=25\), 33, 40 and \(45^{\circ}\) which are related to the MOFs molecules. The defined XRD patterns of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{St}\) arch-Hydrogel refer to an amorphous structure (Fig. 2) (Mahadevan et al., 2012). On the other hand, Fig. S3 shows the change in XRD pattern \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{St}\) arch-Hydrogel after FLV adsorption.
Commonly, the surface area of adsorbent as well as pore size play a significant function in the adsorption and removal processes of adsorbate such as drug molecules (Rajput et al., 2016). Fig. S4 demonstrates the adsorption-desorption surface area of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel. The high characterized surface area \((528.39\;\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\) of this MOFs-hydrogel nanosorbent assists its removal performance. The sufficient preparation of mesoporous \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel was confirmed by the pore size \(2.90\;\mathrm{nm}\) which is categorized as a mesoporous material \((2{-}50\ \mathrm{nm})\), while the pore volume was found \(3.77\;\mathrm{cm}^{3}/\mathrm{g}\).
3.3. Adsorption experiment for FLV statin drug removal by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel
## 3.3.1. pH impact
The removal of FLV statin drug by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel was at most controlled by the influence of solution pHs. As shown in Fig. S5, the removal of FLV statin drug was decreased when the pH increased from 2 to 10 at the different monitored drug concentrations. The maximum removal efficiency of FLV statin drug were 56.25, 63.25 and \(72.09\%\) in acidic medium (pH 2), while the minimum removal efficiency were 12.25, 19.25 and \(28.15\%\) at basic medium (pH 10), Table S3 compiled the absorbance before and after FLV adsorption.
On the other hand, the pzc has an important influence on the adsorption of FLV statin drug onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel (pzc=4). Generally, the surface of investigated nanocomposite was found in different forms according to its pzc, where i) carry a positive charge when pH is lower than its pzc, ii) neutral at pH equal to its pzc and iii) carry a negatively charged surface when pH is higher than its pzc (Mahmoud et al., 2020a). The outlined behaviors can be used to explain the repulsion occurred between the surface of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel and FLV statin drug (pka= 4.56) to inspire the decrease of removal
efficiency of FLV statin drug as demonstrated in Fig. S5 (Shah et al., 2018). On the other hand, three types of bonding are expected to take place in this system between FLV and \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel. (i) H-bonding between carboxylic and OH groups (physisorption), (ii) covalent bond formation between carboxylic and OH groups for the formation of ester group (chemisorption) and (iii) coordinate bond formation between oxygen donor atoms in FLV drug and \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{II})\) ion in \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel (chemisorption) as illustrated in Scheme S3.
## 3.3.2. Time and kinetics study
The impact of contact time between \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel and investigated drug, FLV statin was studied and controlled as a significant factor for monitoring and evaluating the kinetic modeling. Generally, the increase of contact time caused increasing in the drug removal due to progress of the interaction between \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel and FLV statin drug. As shown in Fig. 3a, the minimum values of FLV statin drug removal were 25.07, 32.39 and \(40.85\%\) using 10 min for concentration 15, 25 and \(40\;\mathrm{mg\;L^{-1}}\), respectively. On the other hand, the removal efficiency was gradually increased till the equilibrium between FLV statin drug for full occupation of the surface active sites of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel. The maximum obtained values for removal efficiency were 55.11, 62.08 and \(70.95\%\) at 180 min by 15, 25 and \(40\;\mathrm{mg\;L^{-1}}\), respectively (El-Sigeny et al., 2018).
The rate of FLV statin drug adsorption by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel was investigated by four different kinetic models including pseudo-first order, pseudo-second, liquid film as well as pore film diffusion (Mahmoud et al., 2020a). The pseudo-first model proposes the adsorption of one drug molecule (FLV statin drug) onto an active site of adsorbent \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel).
\[
\ln{\left(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}-\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}\right)}=\ln{\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}-\mathbf{k}_{1}\mathbf{t}}
\]
Where \(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{e}}\) is the adsorbed FLV statin drug at equilibrium \((\mathrm{mg/g})\), \(\mathbf{q}_{\mathrm{t}}\) is the adsorbed FLV statin drug at time \(\mathrm{t}\) \((\mathrm{mg/g})\), \(\mathbf{k}_{1}\) is rate constant \((\mathrm{min}^{-1})\). The \(\mathbf{R}^{2}\) values for FLV statin drug were found 0.8849, 0.8661 and 0.8661 using 15, 25 and \(40\,\mathrm{mg\,L^{-1}}\), respectively. On the other hand, the calculated values of capacity were 358.99, 482.08 and \(502.93\,\mathrm{mg\,g^{-1}}\) at 15, 25 and \(40\,\mathrm{mg\,L^{-1}}\), respectively which are totally different from the determined experimental capacities 576.52, 608.33 and 782.05 \(\mathrm{mg\,g^{-1}}\), respectively. This behavior gives a good indication for unsuitability of the pseudo-first to describe the adsorption of FLV statin drug by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch-Hydrogel}\) (Wu et al., 2002).
The pseudo-second kinetic proposes the adsorption of one drug molecule onto two active sites of hydrogel.
\[
\mathrm{t/q_t}=1/\mathrm{k_2q_e}^2+\mathrm{t/q_e}
\]
The \(\mathbf{R}^{2}\) values for FLV statin drug for the investigated concentrations 15, 25 and \(40\,\mathrm{mg\,L^{-1}}\) were the same and equal to 0.9997 (Fig. 3b). Otherwise, the calculated capacities were 577.05, 599.32 and \(780.35\,\mathrm{mg\,g^{-1}}\) using 15, 25 and \(40\,\mathrm{mg\,L^{-1}}\), which are very close to the experimental capacity 576.52, 608.33 and \(782.05\,\mathrm{mg\,g^{-1}}\) to refer that the pseudo-second order is a suitable kinetic model describing the adsorption of FLV statin drug by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch-Hydrogel}\) (Wu et al., 2002).
The liquid-film diffusion assumes that the rate-determining step in the adsorption process between drug molecules and hydrogel is the flow of FLV statin drug from the liquid-film onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch-Hydrogel}\). This model is described by Eq. (5),
\[
\ln(-\mathrm{F})=-\mathrm{k_{d}t}
\]
The detected low values of \(\mathbf{R}^{2}\) (0.8874, 0.8777 and 0.8874) by 15, 20 and 40 mg L\(^{-1}\), respectively as well as the zero intercept indicate that the liquid-film kinetic model is not the proper one to explain the adsorption steps of FLV statin drug onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel (Mahmoud et al., 2020a). Finally, the adsorption of FLV statin drug was also studied and monitored by pore diffusion kinetic model. This model assumes that the adsorption process depends on the equilibrium interaction between the pore of adsorbent and the adsorbate molecules.
\[
\mathrm{Log}\log\left(\mathrm{C_i}/\left(\mathrm{C_i-q_tm}\right)\right)=\log\left(\mathrm{K_o}/2.303\mathrm{V}\right)\alpha\log\left(\mathrm{t}\right)
\]
The \(\mathbf{R}^{2}\) values for FLV statin drug removal by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel were 0.9166, 0.9166 and 0.9115 using 15, 20 and 40 mg L\(^{-1}\), respectively to assert that the pore diffusion is a less suitable model to represent this process (Mahmoud et al., 2020a).
The high \(\mathbf{R}^{2}\) values (Table 1) and the good agreement between the experimental and calculated capacity (Fig. 3b) confirmed the suitability of pseudo-second kinetics to describe this type of adsorption process.
## 3.3.3. \(\mathbf{NFe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{GA})/\mathbf{Starch}\)-Hydrogel dose impact
The dosage of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel is also known as an important parameter to monitor and optimize the adsorption behavior for drug removal. As demonstrated in Fig. S6, it can be seen that the augmentation in the removal efficiency of FLV statin drug by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel when the dose of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel was increased. The minimum adsorption of FLV statin drug were 11.33, 16.07 and 25.19% using 5 mg for concentration 15, 25 and 40 mg L\(^{-1}\), respectively. On the other hand, the maximum removals were 98.24, 89.0 and 84.88% at 100 mg for concentration 15, 25 and 40 mg L\(^{-1}\), respectively. Commonly, the maximum removal of FLV statin drug onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel refers to more surface functional groups that
are available to interact with FLV statin drug and therefore increase the adsorption efficiency (Zhou et al., 2017).
## 3.3.4. FLV statin drug concentration impact and isotherm study
As shown in Fig. 4a, the impact of FLV statin drug concentration on the removal by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel was investigated under different temperature condition (25, 35 and \(45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)). It was clarified that the removal efficiency of FLV was increased while the FLV statin drug increased from 5 to 50 mg L\(^{-1}\) (Ribeiro et al., 2019). The maximum values for achieved removal efficiency were 92.08, 94.66 and 97.04% at 25, 35 and \(45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively using 50 mg L\(^{-1}\). The elevation of FLV statin drug removal with increasing the initial concentration was mainly caused as a result of the available large number of FLV statin molecules on the surface of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel, which fundamentally resulted in improvement of the adsorption process (Mahmoud and Mohamed., 2018).
The Langmuir model assumes the adsorption of drug molecules on a homogenous surface of the \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel to form surface monolayer by applying Eq. (7).
\[
\mathrm{C_e/q_e=1/q_{max}(b)+C_e/q_{max}}
\]
The \(\mathbf{R}^{2}\) values were all corresponding to 0.9991 at temperature 25, 35 and \(45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). On the other hand, the maximum capacity values were 576.52, 608.33 and 782.05 mg g\(^{-1}\) at 25, 35 and \(45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). As discussed above, the factor of temperature has a positive effect on the adsorption of FLV statin drug onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel. It is evident that the Langmuir model is mostly fitted to explain the adsorption of FLV statin drug onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel (Fig. 4b).
The Freundlich isotherm proposes the adsorption of the desired drug molecules on a heterogeneous surface of adsorbent by multilayer formation of the FLV statin drug on the surface of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel. The linear form of Freundlich isotherm was investigated by applying Eq. (8) and the parameters of this model are compiled in Table 2. The \(\mathbf{R}^{2}\) values of FLV statin drug were 0.9681, 0.9665 and 0.9681 at 25, 35 and \(45^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\), respectively which confirm that the FLV adsorption onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}\)-Hydrogel was less favored by the Freundlich isotherm (Mahmoud et al., 2020a).
\[
\log{\mathrm{q_e}}=\log{\mathrm{K_F}}+1/\mathrm{n}\log{\mathrm{C_e}}
\]
The Dubinin-Radushkevich (D-R) isotherm equation is more general than the Langmuir model because it does not assume a homogeneous surface or a constant sorption potential or absence of steric hindrance between sorbed and incoming particles. The D-R isotherm equation was used to determine the mean free energy of adsorption. The linear form of this model is represented by following Eq. (9) (Zhuang et al., 2019).
\[
\ln{\mathrm{q_e}}=\ln{\mathrm{q_s}}-\mathrm{k_{ad}}\varepsilon^2
\]
The Temkin isotherm model contains a factor that explicitly takes into account the adsorbing species and adsorbate interactions. By ignoring the extremely low and large value of concentrations, the model assumes that heat of adsorption of all molecules in the layer would decrease linearly rather than logarithmic with coverage. As implied in the equations, its derivation is characterized by a uniform distribution of binding energies, up to some maximum binding energy. The linear Temkin isotherm model is represented by Eq. (10) (Mahmoud, Mohamed, 2019b).
\[
\mathrm{q_e=B\ln A_T+B\ln C_e}
\]
Finally, it can be concluded that the Langmuir is the most valid isotherm model to describe the FLV statin drug adsorption onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch-Hydrogel}\) as a result of the correlation coefficient \((\mathbf{R}^{2})\) values at different investigated reaction temperatures as demonstrated in Table 2.
## 3.3.5. Ionic strength impact
The efficiency of FLV statin drug removal by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch-Hydrogel}\) was also monitored in presence of distinct electrolytes as chloride, carbonate, nitrate, bicarbonate, phosphate and sulfate solutions \((0.1\;\mathrm{mol}\;\mathrm{L}^{-1})\). The presence of electrolyte caused a slight decrease in the removal efficiency of FLV statin drug by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch-Hydrogel}\) as demonstrated in Fig. S7. The adsorption efficiency decreased from \(65.14\;\%\) to 62.05, 50.74, 62.65, 60.07, 63.18 and \(48.79\;\%\) in the presence of chloride, carbonate, nitrate, bicarbonate, phosphate and sulfate, respectively for concentration \(15\;\mathrm{mg\;L^{-1}}\) of FLV statin drug. The same behavior was recognized for the other evaluated FLV statin drug concentrations (25 and \(40\;\mathrm{mg\;L^{-1}}\)) as illustrated (Fig. S7). The recognized adsorption values obtained from this study concluded that the \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch-Hydrogel}\) is an efficient adsorbent for FLV statin drug removal in the presence of different electrolyte solutions (Wang et al., 2018).
## 3.3.6. Temperature impact and thermodynamic study
Fig. 5a demonstrates that the removal of FLV statin drug was increased from 52.56 to \(59.15\%\), 56.31 to \(64.84\%\) and 62.21 to \(68.07\%\) for concentration 15, 25 and \(40\;\mathrm{mg\;L^{-1}}\), respectively when the temperature elevated from 25 to \(55^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The influence of temperature on the removal efficiency of FLV statin drug by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch-Hydrogel}\) is considered as a good indicator for the nature of adsorption process either endothermic or exothermic. On the other hand, the elevation of removal efficiency of FLV statin drug is a good indication that this reaction is an endothermic (Wang et al., 2018).
The thermodynamic parameters \(\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}\), \(\Delta\mathrm{G}^{\circ}\) and \(\Delta\mathrm{S}^{\circ}\) were computed to investigate the endothermic nature of this adsorption process as well as to examine the degree of spontaneity according to Eqs. (11-12) and the Van't Hoff plotting (Fig. 5b).
\[
\Delta\mathrm{G}^{\circ}=\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}-\mathrm{T}\Delta\mathrm{S}^{\circ}
\]
\[
\ln\mathrm{K}=-\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}/\mathrm{R}(1/\mathrm{T})+\Delta\mathrm{S}^{\circ}/\mathrm{R}
\]
Where \(\Delta\mathrm{G}^{\circ}\) is the free energy \((\mathrm{J}/\mathrm{mol})\), \(\Delta\mathrm{S}^{\circ}\) is the entropy change \((\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{K})\) and \(\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}\) \((\mathrm{kJ}/\mathrm{mol})\) is the standard enthalpy. Moreover, the obtained thermodynamic parameters from this study are compiled in Table S4. The values of \(\Delta\mathrm{G}^{\circ}\) were in the range (-45.59 to -50.99), (-56.57 to -63.17) and (-64.28 to -71.78 KJmol\(^{-1}\)) at concentration 15, 25 and \(45\ \mathrm{mg\ L^{-1}}\), respectively to imply that the adsorption of FLV statin drug \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}-\mathrm{Hydrogel}\) is a spontaneous process (Mahmoud, Mohamed, 2019b). The positive values of \(\Delta\mathrm{H}^{\circ}\) (8.05, 8.99 and 10.22 \(\mathrm{kJmol}^{-1}\)) at 15, 25 and \(45\ \mathrm{mg\ L^{-1}}\), respectively emphasize the endothermic nature of FLV statin drug adsorption onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}-\mathrm{Hydrogel}\). Finally, the \(\Delta\mathrm{S}^{\circ}\) values (0.18, 0.22 and \(0.25\ \mathrm{Jmol}^{-1}\mathrm{K}^{-1}\)) at 15, 25 and \(45\ \mathrm{mg\ L^{-1}}\), respectively assert the increased randomness during the FLV statin drug adsorption onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}-\mathrm{Hydrogel}\).
The removal processes of pharmaceuticals are mainly affected by adsorbent properties (i.e., surface area, pore size distribution, and surface functional groups), characteristic properties of adsorbate (pka) as well as experimental conditions (pH, time, mass, concentration, temperature). The concluded removal capacities of FLV statin drug onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\mathrm{Starch}-\mathrm{Hydrogel}\) \((782.05\ \mathrm{mg\ g^{-1}})\), \(\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})\) \((534.09\ \mathrm{mg\ g^{-1}})\) and \(\mathrm{Starch}-\mathrm{Hydrogel}\) \((212.67\ \mathrm{mg\ g^{-1}})\) at optimum conditions (pH = 4, time = 30 min, concentration = 40 mg \(\mathrm{L}^{-1}\), temperature \(25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\)) strongly affirm the enhancement caused by the encapsulation of MOFs molecules into polymeric starch hydrogel matrices.
3.3.7. Application of \(\mathbf{NFe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel for FLV removal from real water matrices
The removal of FLV statin drug from different water samples by the action of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel was examined at the selected optimum conditions as compiled in Table S5. The percentage values of FLV statin drug removal from tap water were 98.74, 98.91 and \(99.99\;\%\) using 15, 25 and \(40\;\mathrm{mg\;L^{-1}}\), respectively. Furthermore, the removal values from sea water (96.15, 97.08 and \(98.52\;\%\) ) and industrial wastewater (98.13, 97.92 and \(98.92\;\%\) ) using 15, 25 and \(40\;\mathrm{mg\;L^{-1}}\), respectively.
## 3.4. Optimization of swelling ratio at distinct conditions
The swelling ratio (SRO) is a remarkable factor used to monitor the capacity of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel to absorb water. Additionally, the SRO has a significant effect on the adsorption efficiency of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel for FLV statin drug removal (Fosso-Kankeu, et al., 2019). Fig. 6 represents the swelling ratio at different conditions, viz., time, pH and temperature. The increase in time has mainly caused an obvious increase in the SRO (Fig. 6a) due to the positive effect on the swelling of internal pores of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel as well as surface swelling. Moreover, the SRO of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel was increased from 500 to \(1000\%\) when the solution pH was increased by increasing the pH value from 2 to 10. Generally, at low pH, the high hydrogen ions caused an increasing behavior in the physical cross-linking to lead to shrinking of hydrogel (decrease swelling). On the other hand, at high pH, the high hydroxyl groups in solution were forced to repulse with the hydroxyl groups on the hydroxyl surface to cause noticeable increase in the SRO (Sadasivuni et al., 2016). Finally, the SRO was found to increase from 500 to \(800\%\) when the temperature increased from 25 to \(80^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) (Fig. 6c). This behavior may be related to the disturbance in the H-bonding between the
polymeric hydrogel networks due to increasing in the temperature and thus enhancing the SRO (Rao et al., 2017).
3.5. Regeneration of \(\mathbf{NFe}_{3}\mathbf{O}_{4}@\mathbf{Zn}(\mathbf{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel for additional reuse.
The recycling of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel is a remarkable factor to investigate the economical application of this nanocomposite in FLV statin drug removal from aqueous solution. The loaded FLV onto \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel was rinsed with a regenerating reagent as HCl \((0.1\ \mathrm{mol\ L^{-1}})\) (Liao and Huang, 2016) and the adsorption capacity of the recycled \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel was successively examined for five recycles. Fig. S8 illustrates that the removal capacity of FLV statin by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel was slightly decreased from 782.45 to 700.09 mg g\(^{-1}\) after five regeneration cycles to confirm the validity of multi-step applications of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel.
## 4. Conclusion
A magnetic MOFs-Starch hydrogel was designed and synthesized based on cross-linking of starch hydrogel with MOFs, while the magnetic iron oxide nanoparticles were doped into the MOFs-based hydrogel matrix. The maximum swelling ratio \(1000.0\%\) was established at pH 10, 180 min and \(25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The assembled \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel was optimized to evaluate removal of FLV statin drug from aqueous solution. The adsorption of FLV statin drug by \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel reached equilibrium only after 30 min with maximum adsorption capacity \((782.05\ \mathrm{mg\ g^{-1}})\). The Langmuir isotherm model and pseudo-second order kinetics were correlated well with the experimental adsorption data according to the characterized correlation coefficient values 0.9991 and 0.9997, respectively. The \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel exhibited excellent regeneration ability even after five regeneration cycles. On the other hand, the efficiency and superiority of \(\mathrm{NFe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{GA})/\) Starch-Hydrogel for drug
removal was illustrated and compared with other adsorbent materials as compiled in Table 3.
